# Common loon

The common loon breeds on clear, deep lakes across Canada, the northern United
States, Greenland and Iceland. It takes perch, sucker and crayfish by descending
from the surface and propelling itself through the water after them, driven by
large webbed feet set at the very rear of the body. Bones heavier than those of
most flying relatives, and the ability to squeeze air out of the plumage, let it
sink almost without a ripple. Its yodel carries for kilometres over still water.
See [[gaviidae]].
